p = S4["pooled"]; A(f"\\midrule Pooled & {mn(p['a_ndf']['pooled'])} & {mn(p['b_ndf']['pooled'])} & {mn(p['a_bhatt']['pooled'])} ($I^2$ {100*p['a_bhatt']['I2']:.0f}\\%) & {mn(p['b_bhatt']['pooled'])}\\\\\\bottomrule\\end{{tabular}}")
A(r"""

Faithfulness correlation (FC) is the correlation, over 50 random subsets of $\lceil d/4\rceil$ features, between the summed attribution and the drop in prediction under marginal removal. It is not gated on $G$. Its pooled difference is small and heterogeneous: it is significantly negative on Cleveland, Mammographic, CTG and CDC and significantly positive on DR Debrecen, where NDF shows the largest deficit. The two metrics weigh different things: FC rewards a linear relation between attribution mass and output change over random subsets, whereas NDF rewards removing the prediction quickly with the top-ranked features; on DR Debrecen, where many features are near-duplicates, HE spreads attribution over correlated counts, which can raise the subset-sum correlation while slowing top-$k$ deletion.""")
# S8 mechanism
q = S6["pooled"]; cd = S6["per_dataset"]["cdc_diabetes"]["d_na"]; mo = S6["mechanism_ols"]
A(r"\section*{S8. Pre-specified non-additivity hypothesis (not supported)}")
A(f"""The non-additivity index NA is the mean over 20 random feature pairs of $|D(\\{{j,l\\}})-D(\\{{j\\}})-D(\\{{l\\}})|$ under marginal deletion. We pre-specified (protocol hypotheses H6c and H6c$'$ in the repository) that HE would have higher NA than S* and that patients with larger $\\Delta$NA would show a more negative $\\Delta$NDF. Pooled $\\Delta$NA was {mn(q['d_na']['pooled'],4)} ({pe(q['d_na']['p'])}); on CDC Diabetes, where the deficit appeared without strong correlation, $\\Delta$NA was {mn(cd['mean_diff'],4)} ({pe(cd['p'])}). The patient-level association went in the opposite direction (coefficient {mo['coef']:+.2f}, {pe(mo['p'])}); both NA and NDF are normalized by $G$, which may induce a positive association. The hypothesis is therefore not supported as a general explanation.""")
